course: dictionary
lesson: 10140
title: Thrilled, and what it really means
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Thrilled» means very happy and excited about something. «Toad is thrilled: he will tell them a story, get a lift, and perhaps even drive up to Toad Hall in a car.» The good news tells you he is delighted, so thrilled must mean very happy.
explain: Thrilled can also be the past form of the verb thrill, which means to give someone a strong feeling of excitement. «The ride thrilled us» means the ride made us feel very excited. Look at the words around it to see which meaning fits.
explain: Tone matters too. Said with a flat voice, «Oh, I'm thrilled» can be sarcastic, and it means the opposite of what the words say. The situation and the way it is said are your clues.
fact: Thrilled means very happy and excited about something.
fact: As a verb, thrill means to give someone a strong feeling of excitement, and thrilled is its past form.
fact: «I'm thrilled» is a strong way of saying «I'm very pleased».
fact: Useful phrases are «thrilled to bits», «thrilled with», «thrilled about» and «thrilled to hear».
fact: «Thrilled and scared at once» shows that you can feel two strong feelings together.
exercise: «Poppy gives the whole class designer pencils for Christmas; some kids are thrilled.» What does thrilled mean here? | Hungry for lunch | Bored and tired | *Very happy and excited | Cross and angry
exercise: «The ride thrilled us.» Here thrilled is a verb. What does it mean? | *Gave us a strong feeling of excitement | Made us feel cross | Took us to the shops | Made us feel sleepy
exercise: Which phrase is a strong way of saying «I'm very pleased»? | *I'm thrilled | I'm lost | I'm tired | I'm hungry
test: «Toad is thrilled: he will tell them a story, get a lift, and perhaps even drive up to Toad Hall in a car.» Why is Toad thrilled? | *He gets a story to tell, a lift, and maybe a car ride | He has lost his favourite hat | He has to go to school early | He has to walk home in the rain
test: Dad spills his drink and says in a flat voice, «Oh, I'm thrilled.» What does he probably mean? | *He is not pleased at all | He is thrilled to bits | He is feeling sleepy | He is over the moon
exam: Which sentence uses thrilled as the past form of the verb thrill? | *The news thrilled the whole village. | She is thrilled with her new bike. | We are thrilled to hear the results. | Kai feels thrilled about the trip.
